After each analytical task with a registered selection, respondents are asked whether they would vote for the candidate they have just chosen in a real election. Those answering affirmatively identify the principal considerations underlying their choice. The eligible response options are randomized within each task, with ``Otra raz\'on'' always placed last. The questions themselves retain a fixed order. The factor list includes appearance-related considerations, while ideology and thematic priorities appear only if at least one candidate description was revealed. Perceived time pressure is measured with the same seven-point item in \armT\ and \armB, regardless of the answer to the real-election support question. Perceived effort is measured on a seven-point scale in \armC\ only when the respondent revealed information. Tasks that end without a selection skip every follow-up question.

\subsection{Experimental conditions}

Let $Z_i\in\{T,C,B\}$ denote assignment to one of three decision environments. In \armT, each task includes a visible nine-second countdown, while the candidate descriptions can be opened without an arithmetic requirement. In \armC, there is no deadline, but revealing each candidate's description requires correctly solving a simple addition problem. In \armB, neither a deadline nor an arithmetic barrier applies. Allocation occurs with equal probability at the respondent level when study sessions are initialized and remains concealed through screening and the baseline questionnaire. The candidates, number of tasks, and choice prompt are otherwise held constant. This structure distinguishes two constraints on political learning. \armT\ limits the time available to acquire and integrate information, whereas \armC\ increases the effort required to obtain it. In every condition, decision timing begins only when both photographs have loaded and decoded successfully and are revealed together. Loading time is recorded separately, and an image-loading failure prevents the task from starting until the images are available. The same nine-second deadline applies during practice and analytical tasks in \armT.\footnote{The assigned deadline is retained in browser storage when available. Participants are explicitly warned against refreshing or using browser history. Detected violations terminate participation and exclude the case from the analytical sample, while preserving previously recorded responses and the reason for termination. Stimulus readiness, deadline expiry, response preservation, and navigation handling were verified through isolated simulations and browser tests.} If the deadline expires, the current selection is submitted automatically. When no candidate has been selected, the choice remains missing.

\subsection{Temporal scarcity and deadline calibration}

The time-pressure manipulation is calibrated to the demands of the decision task rather than to a universal threshold for rapid judgment. Ord\'o\~nez and Benson (1997, 122) distinguish the existence of a deadline from its psychological consequences, noting that ``it is possible to have time constraint but no time pressure.'' A consequential deadline must therefore restrict the time respondents would otherwise use to acquire and evaluate information. Experimental evidence shows that temporal constraints can induce faster processing, more selective information search, and changes in decision strategy (Payne, Bettman, and Johnson 1988). Although Ballew and Todorov (2007) elicited electorally predictive competence judgments under a two-second deadline, their task involved photographs alone. The present task additionally permits respondents to reveal and compare political descriptions. Across the available candidate pairs, these descriptions contain a median of 28 words in total. Brysbaert's (2019) estimated Spanish silent-reading rate of 278 words per minute implies approximately six seconds of uninterrupted reading, before allowing for information access, comparison, and candidate selection. This calculation provides context rather than a minimum comprehension time. Moreover, cursor movement and target selection depend on interface geometry and input devices, making fixed allowances per click inappropriate (MacKenzie 1992). The relevant benchmark is consequently respondents' observed performance on the experimental task itself.

The nine-second deadline is informed jointly by research on task-relative time constraints and evidence from a pre-test of the experimental task. The pre-test evaluated an exploratory twenty-second deadline against an unconstrained baseline to assess how strongly that ceiling restricted task-completion time. The pre-test comprised \Sexpr{pretest$n_respondents} eligible completed interviews, contributing \Sexpr{pretest$n_tasks} observations across \Sexpr{pretest$n_main_tasks} analytical tasks per respondent. Figure~\ref{fig:task-duration-completes} reports mean task duration by task position among these completed pre-test participants. Across these tasks, pooled mean duration was \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", pretest$baseline$mean_seconds)} seconds (SD \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", pretest$baseline$sd_seconds)}, \Sexpr{pretest$baseline$n_respondents} respondents and \Sexpr{pretest$baseline$n_tasks} task observations) in the unconstrained condition and \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", pretest$time$mean_seconds)} seconds (SD \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", pretest$time$sd_seconds)}, \Sexpr{pretest$time$n_respondents} respondents and \Sexpr{pretest$time$n_tasks} task observations) under the exploratory \Sexpr{pretest$deadline_seconds}-second deadline. The arithmetic-cost condition contributed the remaining \Sexpr{pretest$cost$n_respondents} respondents and \Sexpr{pretest$cost$n_tasks} task observations. The corresponding unconstrained median was \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", pretest$baseline$median_seconds)} seconds (interquartile range \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", pretest$baseline$q25_seconds)}--\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", pretest$baseline$q75_seconds)}), and only \Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", pretest$baseline$percent_over_deadline)} percent of unconstrained tasks (\Sexpr{pretest$baseline$n_over_deadline} of \Sexpr{pretest$baseline$n_tasks}) lasted longer than \Sexpr{pretest$deadline_seconds} seconds. These observations suggest that the exploratory ceiling frequently exceeded the time respondents used without a deadline, although they do not establish an absence of experienced pressure. To impose a more binding restriction, we adapt the calibration principle employed by Kocher et al. (2019, 220--221), whose deadlines represented ``a 20\% reduction of the median decision times in the absence of constraints.'' Their restrictions applied to blocks of choices rather than individual tasks, so the present design adopts their relative calibration principle rather than their implementation. Applying that reduction to the unconstrained median yields \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", pretest$reference_seconds)} seconds, rounded upward to nine seconds, approximately \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", pretest$reduction_percent)} percent below the observed median. Rounding upward retains a modest margin for information access and response registration while keeping the deadline below typical unconstrained task duration. Although nine seconds exceeds the unconstrained median time to first selection of \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", pretest$baseline$first_selection_median)} seconds, it remains below the typical time used to complete the full task. This restriction therefore compresses opportunities for information acquisition and comparison without assuming that political information becomes inaccessible. Its effectiveness will be assessed jointly through reported hurry, information acquisition, and nonselection at the deadline, rather than inferred from mechanically shortened task durations alone. These completion-conditioned pre-test statistics inform calibration but do not establish that nine seconds is optimal.

\subsection{Outcomes and measurement}

The primary outcomes are recorded candidate selection, whether any description is revealed, the number of descriptions revealed, time to first selection capped at a common nine-second window, and failure to register a selection within that window. Description revelation is the observed measure of information acquisition and does not establish that the text was read or understood. These candidate-specific records permit tests of gendered scrutiny. Total time from photograph display to task submission, changes in selection, and information-button presses are recorded separately. Information-acquisition measures cover the entire task, including openings after an initial selection. Terminal nonselection at the deadline is retained as a separate outcome. Post-choice measures capture whether respondents would support the selected candidate in a real election and which considerations they report using. The candidate profiles include gender, age, party affiliation, geographic constituency, electoral performance, political information, and previously measured facial characteristics. Recording the alternatives and information available in each task allows us to distinguish treatment effects on information acquisition from treatment effects on recorded selection.

\section{Potential outcomes and estimands}

\subsection{Notation and common averaging distribution}

For eligible respondent $i$ in analytical task $t$, let $Z_i\in\{T,C,B\}$ denote the assigned decision environment and let $P_{it}=(C^L_{it},C^R_{it})$ denote the ordered candidate pair. For side $s\in\{L,R\}$, define $M^s_{it}(z,p)\in\{0,1\}$ as the potential indicator that the respondent reveals the political description of the candidate on side $s$ at any point before the task ends under environment $z$ and pair $p$. This measures access to the text, without establishing that it was read or understood. Respondents may reveal information after an initial selection and revise that selection before submission. Aggregate information acquisition is defined as
\[
K_{it}(z,p)=M^L_{it}(z,p)+M^R_{it}(z,p),\quad
A_{it}(z,p)=\mathbf 1\{K_{it}(z,p)\geq 1\},\quad
X_{it}(z,p)=\mathbf 1\{K_{it}(z,p)=2\}.
\]
Thus, $K$ is the number of descriptions revealed, $A$ indicates access to at least one description, and $X$ indicates access to both descriptions.

Let $T^*_{it}(z,p)$ denote seconds from the simultaneous display of both fully loaded photographs to the first candidate selection, with $T^*=\infty$ if no selection occurs. To place all conditions on a common observation window, define capped selection latency $D^{9}_{it}(z,p)=\min\{T^*_{it}(z,p),9\}$ and slow or absent selection $S^{9}_{it}(z,p)=\mathbf 1\{T^*_{it}(z,p)>9\}$. If no selection occurs within nine seconds, $D^{9}=9$ and $S^{9}=1$. This common analytical window does not impose a deadline on the baseline or arithmetic-cost conditions, whose uncapped response times remain recorded. The full task duration, measured from photograph display to submission, is recorded separately and can include information acquisition and revisions after the first selection. Loading time precedes both measures. Let $Q_{it}(z,p)$ indicate that a task terminates at the deadline without any candidate selection. This outcome is possible under \armT; the other conditions require a selection before submission. Reaching the deadline after selecting a candidate preserves the current selection and gives $Q=0$. Thus, $S^{9}$ measures failure to select within the common window, while $Q$ identifies termination without a candidate response.

Candidate choice is defined without privileging the randomized left position. Let $V_{it}(z,p)$ denote the candidate selected when the task ends; $V=\varnothing$ denotes a missing candidate response when $Q=1$. For each displayed side, define the registered-selection event
\[
Y^s_{it}(z,p)=\mathbf 1\{V_{it}(z,p)=C^s_{it}\}.
\]
When a candidate response is recorded, $Y^L_{it}+Y^R_{it}=1$; when $Q_{it}=1$, both derived event indicators equal zero, while the candidate response itself remains missing. This definition neither imputes a vote nor assigns an unobserved preference to a respondent who made no selection. In the full eligible randomized population, its expectation is the probability that a displayed profile receives a registered selection under the assigned environment. Effects on this outcome can reflect both changes in which candidate is selected and changes in whether any selection is completed.

Unless otherwise stated, the causal estimands below weight eligible respondents and the fifteen analytical tasks equally and average over the randomized candidate draw, pairing, task order, and left-right assignment. Practice tasks are excluded. Comparisons between $z\in\{T,C\}$ and \armB\ define respondent-level intention-to-treat targets. The reported estimates are restricted to completed interviews and must be distinguished from these full-population targets, as detailed in the estimation strategy.

\subsection{Effects of the decision environment}

For each task-level outcome $R\in\{A,K,X,D^{9},S^{9}\}$, define
\[
\tau^R_{zB}=\E\!\left[R_{it}(z,P_{it})-R_{it}(B,P_{it})\right],
\qquad z\in\{T,C\}.
\]
The terminal-timeout effect is defined separately as $\tau^Q_{TB}=\E[Q_{it}(T,P_{it})-Q_{it}(B,P_{it})]$ because forced task termination exists only in \armT. These estimands capture how temporal scarcity and information cost change information acquisition, decision speed, and response completion. Information acquisition is also a potential mediator of candidate choice. Because it occurs after treatment, models that condition choice on realized information acquisition do not identify a controlled or natural direct effect without additional assumptions; mediation analyses are therefore interpreted cautiously and do not carry a direct-effect interpretation.

Randomized pairing and position also identify side-neutral profile-selection probabilities. For complete candidate profile $c$, define
\[
\theta_c(z)=\E\!\left[Y^s_{it}(z,P_{it})\mid C^s_{it}=c\right],\qquad
\Delta_c(z)=\theta_c(z)-\theta_c(B).
\]
The expectation averages over respondents, tasks, randomized rivals, and side. $\Delta_c(z)$ is the causal effect of the decision environment on the probability that profile $c$ is selected when displayed. Contrasts $\theta_c(z)-\theta_{c'}(z)$ compare randomly assigned complete profiles under the same environment. They do not isolate gender, ideology, party, or facial characteristics because those attributes remain bundled within each observed candidate.

\subsection{Gendered information acquisition and choice}

Let $G(c)=1$ denote a profile coded as a woman. For $R\in\{M,Y\}$, define the candidate-level mean, gender-indexed contrast, and treatment-induced change as
\[
\mu^R_g(z)=\E\!\left[R^s_{it}(z,P_{it})\mid G(C^s_{it})=g\right],\quad
\Gamma_R(z)=\mu^R_1(z)-\mu^R_0(z),\quad
\Psi_R(z)=\Gamma_R(z)-\Gamma_R(B).
\]
$\Gamma_M(z)$ compares the probability of consulting political information about women and men candidates, while $\Gamma_Y(z)$ compares their probability of selection. The corresponding $\Psi_M(z)$ and $\Psi_Y(z)$ estimands identify how random assignment to a constrained decision environment changes each gender-indexed contrast. Candidate gender is not independently randomized, so $\Gamma_M(z)$ and $\Gamma_Y(z)$ compare complete profiles grouped by gender and are not causal effects of gender. The causal interpretation of $\Psi_M(z)$ and $\Psi_Y(z)$ is limited to the effect of the randomized environment on those fixed profile-group differences.

\subsection{Hypotheses and analytical priorities}

The \textbf{primary process family} consists of $\tau^A_{zB}$, $\tau^K_{zB}$, $\tau^{D^{9}}_{zB}$, and $\tau^{S^{9}}_{zB}$ for $z\in\{T,C\}$. The \textbf{primary gender-interaction family} consists of $\Psi_M(T)$, $\Psi_M(C)$, $\Psi_Y(T)$, and $\Psi_Y(C)$. The baseline contrasts $\Gamma_M(B)$ and $\Gamma_Y(B)$ form a separate descriptive family because candidate gender is not independently assigned. Hypothesis tests are two-sided even where theory implies a direction. Their empirical counterparts are estimated among completed interviews, so the causal status of the target estimands does not by itself establish a causal interpretation for the reported contrasts.

We expect both constrained conditions to reduce any information acquisition and the number of descriptions consulted, $\tau^A_{zB}<0$ and $\tau^K_{zB}<0$ (H1). We also expect \armT\ to reduce capped selection latency, $\tau^{D^{9}}_{TB}<0$, while effects on $S^{9}$ and the timing effects of \armC\ are tested without directional restrictions (H2). Under \armB, we expect respondents to consult information more often for profiles of women candidates, $\Gamma_M(B)>0$, and both constraints to attenuate this difference, $\Psi_M(T)<0$ and $\Psi_M(C)<0$ (H3). Finally, we expect $\Psi_Y(T)$ and $\Psi_Y(C)$ to differ from zero, with their signs left open because prior work implies both potential penalties and advantages for women candidates (H4).

\textbf{Additional estimands} include acquisition of both descriptions $X$, terminal timeout $Q$, full task duration, profile-specific $\theta_c(z)$ and $\Delta_c(z)$, the real-election support item, self-reported time pressure and information cost, ideological congruence, associations between choice and other candidate characteristics, and moderation by respondent gender or political sophistication. \textbf{Additional analyses} examine mediation through information acquisition, models conditioned on realized information acquisition or completed choice, high-dimensional profile interactions, decision-factor responses, and subgroup variation. Because these analyses require further assumptions or extend beyond the central hypotheses, they will be interpreted separately from the primary tests.

\subsection{Timeout and missing-data rules}

For respondents meeting the interview-completion criteria, all descriptions revealed before a task terminates contribute to $M^s$, $A$, $K$, and $X$, including when no candidate is selected. A task with no selection within nine seconds receives $D^{9}=9$ and $S^{9}=1$. If the \armT\ task terminates without a selection, $Q=1$, the recorded candidate response remains missing, the derived registered-selection indicators satisfy $Y^L=Y^R=0$, and every follow-up question is skipped. These undefined follow-up responses will not be imputed. If a selection has already occurred when the deadline expires, the current candidate response is retained, $Q=0$, and the usual follow-up questions are shown. Up to two missing analytical choices are permitted within a completed interview, and their associated information and timing outcomes remain in the analysis. Participation ends when a third analytical task is submitted without a valid selection, because the minimum response requirement can no longer be met. These respondents, and those who otherwise do not finish the study, are excluded from substantive analyses but remain in recruitment accounting, with their previously recorded responses preserved. Withdrawal or abandonment before task submission is not recoded as a timeout. No missing candidate choice or undefined follow-up response will be imputed.

\section{Estimation strategy}

All substantive analyses are restricted to eligible completed interviews as defined above. Each retained respondent contributes all fifteen analytical tasks, including up to two tasks without a registered candidate selection. Outcomes $A$, $K$, $D^{9}$, and $S^{9}$ are estimated using linear models with indicators for \armT\ and \armC, giving unadjusted contrasts relative to \armB\ within this sample. Let $H_i=1$ indicate that respondent $i$ meets the observed completion criteria. For a task-level outcome $R$, the reported condition contrast is
\[
\delta^R_{zB}=\E[R_{it}\mid Z_i=z,H_i=1]-\E[R_{it}\mid Z_i=B,H_i=1].
\]
Because the decision environment may affect reaching the final screen, recording at least thirteen choices, or detected browser navigation, conditioning on $H_i=1$ can select different respondents across conditions. Consequently, $\delta^R_{zB}$ need not equal the full-population intention-to-treat effect $\tau^R_{zB}$, even under randomized assignment. The reported contrasts are therefore interpreted as completion-conditioned comparisons rather than automatically as causal effects for all eligible randomized respondents. Screening, final-screen arrival, navigation-related exclusions, and other incomplete interviews are reported separately to make this selection transparent. Standard errors are clustered by respondent. Covariate-adjusted specifications using pretreatment characteristics and task-order indicators may improve precision but do not, by themselves, remove completion-related selection.

Candidate-level analyses use two observations per analytical task among completed interviews, one for each displayed candidate, including retained tasks that terminate without a selection. Linear probability models for $M^s$ and the derived registered-selection outcome $Y^s$ include treatment indicators, candidate gender, treatment-by-gender interactions, randomized side, and task-order indicators. Their interaction coefficients are completion-conditioned empirical counterparts of $\Psi_M(z)$ and $\Psi_Y(z)$ and share the same selection limitation. Primary uncertainty for these models uses two-way clustering by respondent and candidate. Profile-specific selection probabilities likewise condition on completed interviews and are not interpreted as attribute-level average marginal component effects.

Supplementary analyses describe which candidate characteristics are associated with registered selection in each decision environment. For gender, displayed ideology, dominance classification, and candidate age category, we report both the proportion selected among displayed profiles and the composition of the selected profiles. The former accounts for how often each characteristic is encountered, whereas the latter describes the candidates respondents choose. A joint linear probability model includes these four classifications, their interactions with the assigned condition, randomized side, and task-order indicators. Within-condition contrasts compare women with men, left- and right-identified profiles with centrist profiles, high with low dominance, and older with younger age categories. These are adjusted associations across observed profile bundles rather than causal effects of independently assigned attributes. The model does not condition on whether respondents opened political information, which is itself a response to the experimental environment. Two-way clustered confidence intervals and unadjusted two-sided tests accompany these exploratory contrasts\ifnum\Sexpr{as.integer(has_model_data)}=1 in Appendix~\ref{app:candidate-characteristics}\fi, separately from the primary hypothesis families.

Within the primary process and gender-interaction families, two-sided $p$-values are adjusted separately by Holm's sequential procedure. The two baseline gender contrasts constitute a third, descriptive multiplicity family. Unadjusted estimates with 95\% confidence intervals are also reported. Pretreatment characteristics and the distribution of candidate profiles and positions are summarized within the completed sample. Manipulation checks examine arithmetic-problem completion, information acquisition, $D^{9}$, $S^{9}$, and $Q$, together with reported hurry in \armT\ relative to \armB\ among tasks with a registered selection. These checks are conditional on interview completion, and the hurry comparison additionally conditions on an observed answer. The timeout frequency among completed interviews excludes respondents with more than two missing choices and therefore does not represent the timeout frequency of all recruits. Task-order analyses also use only retained interviews, while practice tasks remain outside the analytical sample. Candidate gender and respondent gender are distinguished throughout.

