\section{Discussion}

The experiment examines whether randomized constraints alter political information acquisition, candidate choice, and the scrutiny applied to women candidates. The reported comparisons are restricted to eligible completed interviews, so their causal interpretation depends on assumptions about completion-related selection. Because gender, ideology, party, and facial characteristics remain bundled within observed candidates, gender-indexed comparisons additionally capture heterogeneity across naturally occurring profiles rather than isolated attribute effects. Observed information acquisition locates a stage at which unequal evaluation may emerge but does not, by itself, establish an unconscious psychological mechanism. Combining randomized decision environments, repeated choices, and behavioral measures of political learning connects appearance-based judgment to the acquisition and use of substantive information in the Colombian electoral context.

