\section{Candidate characteristics and registered selection}
\label{app:candidate-characteristics}
\renewcommand{\thetable}{B\arabic{table}}
\renewcommand{\thefigure}{B\arabic{figure}}
\setcounter{table}{0}
\setcounter{figure}{0}

\begin{table}[!htbp]
\centering\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\caption{Displayed and selected candidate characteristics by condition}
\label{tab:candidate-trait-composition}
<<table-candidate-trait-composition, results='asis', eval=has_model_data>>=
@
\par\smallskip
\begin{minipage}{0.98\linewidth}\footnotesize
\textit{Notes.} The table includes \Sexpr{paper_counts$candidate_appearances} candidate
appearances from \Sexpr{paper_counts$candidate_profiles} distinct candidates evaluated by
\Sexpr{paper_counts$completed_respondents} eligible completed respondents.
Each submitted analytical task contributes its \Sexpr{profiles_per_task} displayed candidate profiles.
Shown counts profile appearances, and chosen counts registered selections.
Chosen / shown is the percentage of displayed profiles with that characteristic
that were selected. Among chosen is the percentage of registered selections with
that characteristic, calculated separately for each attribute and condition.
The latter percentages therefore sum to 100 within each attribute and condition.
A retained task without a selection contributes \Sexpr{profiles_per_task} shown profiles and no chosen
profile. The original missing choice is not imputed.
Ideology refers to the classification in the candidate's available description.
The age grouping uses the supplied younger/older profile classification rather
than age in years.
The cost condition requires an arithmetic operation to access information.
\end{minipage}
\end{table}

\clearpage
\begin{table}[!htbp]
\centering\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\caption{Adjusted associations between candidate characteristics and selection}
\label{tab:candidate-trait-associations}
<<table-candidate-trait-associations, results='asis', eval=has_model_data>>=
@
\par\smallskip
\begin{minipage}{0.98\linewidth}\footnotesize
\textit{Notes.} Estimates are within-condition differences in the probability
of a registered candidate selection, expressed in percentage points.
The joint linear probability model includes candidate gender, displayed ideology,
dominance classification, and candidate age group, together with their interactions
with condition, randomized side, and task-order indicators.
Reference categories are men, centrist ideology, low dominance, and the younger
age category. Confidence intervals are pointwise \Sexpr{ci_percent}\% intervals with two-way
clustering by respondent and candidate. Two-sided $p$-values are unadjusted
exploratory tests and do not enter the primary multiplicity families.
When required, the software's standard positive-semidefinite covariance correction
is applied.
The model uses \Sexpr{paper_counts$candidate_appearances} candidate appearances from
\Sexpr{paper_counts$completed_respondents} eligible completed interviews. Retained submissions
without a choice contribute zero registered selections for both displayed
candidates, while the original candidate response remains missing.
Because candidate attributes remain bundled within real profiles, these contrasts
are adjusted associations, not attribute-level average marginal component effects.
They do not isolate the effect of reading political information.
\end{minipage}
\end{table}

\clearpage
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
<<figure-candidate-trait-associations, fig.width=6.7, fig.height=5.8, eval=has_model_data>>=
@
\caption{Candidate characteristics and registered selection by decision environment}
\label{fig:candidate-trait-associations}
\par\smallskip
\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}\footnotesize
\textit{Notes.} Points report adjusted within-condition differences in registered
selection in percentage points from the joint model in
Table~\ref{tab:candidate-trait-associations}. Bars show two-way clustered pointwise
\Sexpr{ci_percent}\% confidence intervals. The vertical line marks no difference from the relevant
reference category. Estimates use \Sexpr{paper_counts$candidate_appearances} candidate
appearances from \Sexpr{paper_counts$completed_respondents} eligible completed interviews.
The layout follows a conjoint coefficient plot, but the estimates are associations
across naturally occurring candidate profiles rather than causal effects of
independently randomized attributes. Baseline has no time or arithmetic constraint,
time imposes a \Sexpr{deadline_seconds}-second deadline, and cost requires an arithmetic operation.
\end{minipage}
\end{figure}

